lection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: 【0†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org】 Report Issue 【1†Back to Abstract】 【2†Download PDF】 Contents Reading mode Colour theme\nL13:   1. 【3†Abstract】\nL14:   2. 【4†1 Introduction】\nL15:     1. 【5†1.1 Our Contributions】\nL16:     2. 【6†1.2 Paper Organization】\nL17:   3. 【7†2 Background and Motivation】\nL18:     1. 【8†2.1 Zero-Knowledge Proof Protocols】\nL19:   4. 【9†3 ZKPU Architecture】\nL20:     1. 【10†3.1 Twin Systolic Fabric】\nL21:     2. 【11†3.2 Deque buffer】\nL22:     3. 【12†3.3 ZK Engine】\nL23:     4. 【13†3.4 FIPS Montgomery Multiplication Illustration】\nL24:   5. 【14†4 Accelerating ZKP Protocols】\nL25:     1. 【15†4.1 Curve-based Protocols】\nL26:     2. 【16†4.2 Hash-based Protocols】\nL27:   6. 【17†5 Methodology & Evaluation】\nL28:     1. 【18†5.1 Methodology】\nL29:     2. 【19†5.2 Performance Evaluation.】\nL30:   7. 【20†6 Related Work】\nL31:   8. 【21†7 Conclusion】\nL32:   9. 【22†References】\nL33: 【23†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org】\nL34: \nL35: arXiv:2610.27530v1 [cs.AR] 20 Oct 2026\nL36: # ZKPU: Z ero-K nowledge P rocessing U nit for Heterogeneous Workloads\nL37: \nL38: Jonathan Ku^{1} Note: Correspondence E-mail: jonathan.ku@duke.edu    Changchun Zhou^{1}    Jiawen Wu^{2}    Sangouk Jeon^{3}    Cong Guo^{1}    Yuzhe Fu^{1}    Bowen Duan^{1}    Dongsuk Jeon^{3}    JV Rajendran^{2}    Yiran Chen^{1}    Hai “Helen” Li^{1}\nL39: ^{1}Duke University ^{2}Texas A&M University ^{3}Seoul National University\nL40: ###### Abstract\nL41: Zero-knowledge proofs (ZKPs) have become a fundamental building block for privacy-preserving computation, blockchain systems, and verifiable machine learning. Modern ZKP protocols span two distinct families: curve-based protocols, which rely on large finite-field arithmetic, and hash-based protocols, which operate over native 64-bit fields.\nL42: Existing hardware accelerators are specialized for one protocol family because they are built on operand-scanning Montgomery multiplication, resulting in fixed-width datapaths, poor utilization across heterogeneous operand widths, and limited scalability.\nL43: This paper presents ZKPU, a Zero-Knowledge Processing Unit for heterogeneous ZKP workloads. ZKPU introduces a new compute paradigm by co-designing finely integrated product scanning (FIPS) Montgomery multiplication with a reconfigurable Twin Systolic Fabric (TSF), enabling modular multiplication at arbitrary widths in 64-bit increments.\nL44: Combined with a fine-grained Deque buffer, on-chip twiddle-factor generator, and memory-efficient MSM preprocessing strategy, ZKPU provides a unified architecture for both curve-based and hash-based protocols.\nL45: Evaluation using Groth16 and Plonky2 shows that ZKPU achieves up to $144\\times$ speedup for NTT, $13.6\\times$ for MSM, and approximately $7.2\\times$ end-to-end application speedup over state-of-the-art accelerators, while being the first architecture to efficiently support both protocol families on a single reconfigurable hardware platform.\nL46: Keywords: Curve-based ZKPs, Hash-based ZKPs, Modular Multiplier, FIPS, Systolic Array\nL47: ## 1 Introduction\nL48: Zero-knowledge proofs (ZKPs) [【24†12】] enable a prover to convince a verifier that a statement is true without revealing any information beyond its validity, and have become a fundamental building block for blockchain systems [【25†4】, 【26†24】, 【27†15】], verifiable computation [【28†28】], and machine-learning verification[【29†40】, 【30†20】, 【31†7】], etc [【32†6】]. Practical ZKP protocols broadly fall into two families.\nL49: Curve-based protocols, such as Groth16 [【33†14】] and PLONK [【34†10】], rely on elliptic-curve cryptography (ECC) to generate succinct proofs with fast verification. Their prover execution is dominated by arithmetic-intensive kernels, primarily number-theoretic transforms (NTTs) and multi-scalar multiplication (MSM), over $256\\sim 768$-bit prime fields.\nL50: In contrast, hash-based protocols, including Plonky2 [【35†33】] and Starky [【36†39】], rely on ZKP-friendly hash functions such as Poseidon [【37†13】], eliminating the need for trusted setup while providing post-quantum security. Their prover execution is dominated by NTTs and Merkle-tree construction, where each hash computation operates over the 64-bit Goldilocks field, resulting in computational characteristics that differ fundamentally from curve-based provers. This is illustrated in 【38†Figure 1】.\nL51: Despite their algorithmic diversity, the core kernels of ZKP provers reduce to modular arithmetic. NTT, MSM, and Poseidon hash are all dominated by modular addition and multiplication, with modular multiplication accounting for most of the computation. However, operand bitwidths vary by nearly $12\\times$, ranging from 64-bit Goldilocks fields to 256/384-bit prime fields (BN254 and BLS12-381) and up to 768-bit fields (MNT4-753).\nL52: 【39†Image: Refer to caption】 Figure 1: Curve-based and hash-based ZKP protocols rely on distinct computational kernels and arithmetic primitives, resulting in heterogeneous workloads that motivate the design of ZKPU, a unified processing unit for both protocol families. Table 1: Architectural comparison of representative ZKP acceleration works.\nL53: Work  | Method  | Operand Granularity  | Supported Protocols  | Cryptographic Backend  | Reconfigurability\nL54: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL55: PipeZK  | CIOS  | 256 or 384 or 768-bit  | Groth16 (BN128,BLS12-381,MNT4-753)  | Curve-based  | x\nL56: LegoZK  | CIOS  | 256/384/768-bit  | Groth16 (BN128,BLS12-381,MNT4-753)  | Curve-based  | v\nL57: UniZK  | Classical  | 64-bit  | Plonky2, Starky  | Hash-based  | x\nL58: ZKPU  | FIPS  | 64*n-bit ($n=1...12$)  | Groth16 (BN128,BLS12-381,MNT4-753), Plonky2  | Curve+Hash-based  | v\nL59: Prior works as shown in 【40†Table 1】 scale downward: operand-scanning datapaths designed for the maximum supported operand width can only be partitioned into narrower multipliers, reducing utilization for smaller operands while leaving wider operands unsupported. Runtime width flexibility is therefore limited to downward partitioning.\nL60: For example, LegoZK [【41†38】] employs a reconfigurable PE with a fixed 768-bit interface that can be split into 256- or 384-bit lanes, whereas all other designs fix the operand bitwidth at design or synthesis time. To overcome this limitation, we propose ZKPU, a Zero-Knowledge Processing Unit for Heterogeneous Workloads, which scales upward by composing 64-bit processing units into arbitrary widths in 64-bit increments.\nL61: As a result, a single architecture efficiently supports 64-bit hash-based protocols, $256\\sim 768$-bit curve-based protocols, and future protocols with even wider operands.\nL62: We trace this limitation to a common root cause: existing accelerators are built on operand-scanning Montgomery multiplication (CIOS/FIOS [【42†16】]), whose datapath is inherently tied to the operand bitwidth. This coupling fixes the supported width at design time, increases on-chip operand storage, and limits data reuse.\nL63: To break this dependency, we instead adopt finely integrated product scanning (FIPS) [【42†16】], which operates on 64-bit chunks and naturally composes into modular multipliers of arbitrary widths in 64-bit increments. Although FIPS has long been considered difficult to accelerate because of its irregular operand ordering, we show that these access patterns map efficiently onto a two-dimensional systolic-like architecture, enabling scalable modular arithmetic across heterogeneous ZKP workloads.\nL64: We realize this insight with the Twin Systolic Fabric (TSF), a reconfigurable two-dimensional array of 64-bit processing elements that implements FIPS-based modular multiplication. By composing the same 64-bit building blocks, TSF scales seamlessly from native 64-bit hashing to $256\\sim 768$-bit EC operations without changing the underlying hardware. Sixteen TSF-based ZK Engines, integrated with our proposed fine-grained operand Deque buffer and on-chip twiddle-factor generator, together form ZKPU.\nL65: ### 1.1 Our Contributions\nL66: \nL67:   * •\nL68: \nL69: A width-scalable modular multiplication paradigm. We identify operand-scanning Montgomery multiplication as the root cause of fixed-width ZKP accelerators and introduce a co-designed compute paradigm that combines FIPS with the TSF, enabling modular multiplication at arbitrary widths in 64-bit increments.\nL70: \nL71:   * •\nL72: A unified architecture for heterogeneous ZKP workloads. TSF composes the same 64-bit processing elements into modular multipliers spanning $64\\sim 768$ bits, efficiently supporting both curve-based and hash-based ZKP protocols while improving utilization and reducing operand movement through systolic data reuse.\nL73: \nL74:   * •\nL75: ZKPU and comprehensive evaluation. We implement ZKPU in Verilog using a TSMC 16nm technology and evaluate it under unified scaling methodology. ZKPU achieves up to $16\\times$ speedup over PipeZK and up to $4.3\\times$ over LegoZK, while being able to efficiently support both Groth16 and Plonky2 on a single reconfigurable architecture.\nL76: ### 1.2 Paper Organization\nL77: The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. 【7†Section 2】 reviews ZKP protocols and the background of Montgomery multiplication. 【9†Section 3】 presents the proposed ZKPU architecture, including the Twin Systolic Fabric (TSF) and ZK Engine. 【14†Section 4】 describes the computational characteristics of curve-based and hash-based ZKPs and their mapping onto ZKPU. 【17†Section 5】 details the implementation methodology and experimental evaluation.\nL78: 【20†Section 6】 discusses related work on ZKP accelerators, and 【21†Section 7】 concludes the paper.\nL79: ## 2 Background and Motivation\nL80: ### 2.1 Zero-Knowledge Proof Protocols\nL81: ZKPs enable a prover to convince a verifier of a statement’s validity without revealing any additional information. They have been widely adopted in blockchain systems, cryptocurrencies [【25†4】, 【26†24】, 【27†15】], verifiable machine learning [【29†40】], online auctions [【43†11】], and privacy-preserving computation.\nL82: Among practical constructions, zk-SNARKs [【44†19】, 【28†28】, 【45†8】, 【46†23】] generate succinct proofs with fast verification, whereas zk-STARKs [【47†5】, 【48†3】, 【49†2】] provide transparent setup, post-quantum security, and improved scalability.\nL83: From a computational perspective, ZKP protocols can be broadly classified into two families: curve-based and hash-based. Curve-based protocols rely on ECC to generate proofs, while hash-based protocols employ ZKP-friendly hash functions, such as Poseidon [【37†13】], instead of conventional cryptographic hashes (e.g., SHA-256), which are inefficient for proof generation.\nL84: Although these protocol families differ in their cryptographic primitives, both ultimately reduce to modular arithmetic and share common computational kernels, motivating a unified hardware architecture.\nL85: 【50†Image: Refer to caption】 Figure 2: Example for Montgomery multiplication in CIOS method. 【51†Image: Refer to caption】 Figure 3: Example for Montgomery multiplication in FIPS method on our ZKPU.\nL86: Modular multiplication is the computational bottleneck in many public-key cryptosystems, including ZKP provers. Among existing reduction algorithms, Montgomery multiplication [【52†25】] is the de facto choice because it eliminates costly division operations and efficiently supports repeated modular multiplications.\nL87: To handle large operands ranging from 64 to 768 bits, practical implementations partition operands into multiple machine words and employ multi-word scanning algorithms [【42†16】, 【53†26】, 【54†37】]. Different scanning orders expose distinct dataflows and hardware characteristics. In this work, we focus on operand scanning and finely integrated product scanning (FIPS), and show how co-designing the architecture with FIPS enables efficient, width-scalable modular multiplication.\nL88: Figure 4: Architecture overview of ZKPU. Left: sixteen ZK Engines shared the global buffer, and each engine couples a Deque buffer, an on-chip twiddle factor generator, and 9 reconfigurable TSFs. Middle: TSF is a two-dimensional array of 64-bit twin PEs, where operands $A$, $B$, $m$, and $n$ stream in from the array edges, partial sums accumulate along anti-diagonals, and each PE fuses the product multiply with the Montgomery reduction multiply.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn4view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/THU-HAS/MegaZK/blob/main/LICENSE\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/THU-HAS/MegaZK/blob/main/LICENSE: Cache miss"
